From e4eb3a6b46ce5cdb48118cfca92345232ce60cfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 23:29:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 79/83] libm: accurate large-argument sin/cos/tan (Payne-Hanek
 reduction)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An audit flagged the sin/cos/tan range-reduction loop in math_trig.c as a
critical logic bug ("operates on the result, fprem1 does mod 1, infinite
loop").  That diagnosis is incorrect: when |x| >= 2^63 the x87 fsin/fcos/
fptan set C2 and leave st(0) = the INPUT unchanged, so the loop reduces x
(not the result), and fprem1 with divisor 2pi reduces mod 2pi (not mod 1).
Verified: no hang at any magnitude up to 1e300.

But there IS a real bug the audit's symptom pointed at: the loop reduces
using only an 80-bit value of 2pi, so for large |x| the reduced argument
loses precision — sin(1e15) came out 0.85827213 vs the true Payne-Hanek
0.85827279317023586 (~6e-7 error).  (Easy to miss because i386 glibc's fsin
shares the same limitation, so a -m32 host comparison "agrees".)

Fix: port fdlibm's Payne-Hanek reduction (math_rem_pio2.c: __kernel_rem_pio2
with the exact 2/pi ipio2[] table + __ieee754_rem_pio2 dispatcher with the
Cody-Waite 3-word pi/2 path).  sin/cos/tan keep the bit-for-bit fast x87
path for small |x| (< 2^20*pi/2); for larger |x| they reduce to r in
[-pi/4, pi/4] plus a quadrant and evaluate the accurate hardware fsin/fcos
on the small r.  NaN/Inf handling preserved.

Verified against the true (bigint Payne-Hanek) golden values: sin(1e15),
sin(1e10), sin(1e20) all within 1e-10; cos/tan large-arg likewise; small
args bit-identical to before (0 differences).  tests/lib/m test_trig: the
two large-arg failures are fixed (4 -> 2; the remaining 2 are pre-existing
sinpif/tanpi in the *pi family).  ipio2/npio2_hw/pio2_* constants verified
exact against an independent 2/pi computation.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
